      "text": "To find the remainder when an integer \\( n \\) is divided by 15, we know that the remainder must be less than 15. Given that the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 15 is 7, we can express \\( n \\) as:\n\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nWe need to find the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5. Let's start with the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3.\n\nFirst, consider the remainder when \\( 15k + 7 \\) is divided by 3. Since \\( 15k \\) is divisible by 3, the remainder is the same as the remainder when 7 is divided by 3. We perform the division:\n\n\\[ 7 \\div 3 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\nSo, \\( 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\).\n\nTherefore, when \\( n \\) is divided by 3, the remainder is 1. We express this as:\n\n\\[ n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\n\nNext, let's find the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5. Again, we use the fact that \\( 15k + 7 \\) is divisible by 5, since 15 is divisible by 5. The remainder when \\( 7 \\) is divided by 5 is:\n\n\\[ 7 \\div 5 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\nSo, \\( 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\).\n\nTherefore, when \\( n \\) is divided by 5, the remainder is 2. We express this as:\n\n\\[ n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the sum of the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5. From our previous calculations, we have:\n\n\\[ n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\n\\[ n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nThe sum of these remainders is:\n\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the remainders when the same integer is divided by 3 and by 5 is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{3}\\]",
