      "text": "To find the integer, let's consider both equations:\n\nEquation 1: \\( x \\equiv 7 \\pmod{15} \\)\nThis can be expressed as \\( x = 15 \\cdot y + 7 \\) for some integer \\( y \\).\n\nEquation 2: \\( x \\equiv r_1 \\pmod{3} \\)\nand \\( x \\equiv r_2 \\pmod{5} \\)\nWhere \\( r_1 \\) and \\( r_2 \\) are the remainders when \\( x \\) is divided by 3 and by 5 respectively.\n\nLooking at Equation 1, let's find the remainder of \\( 15 \\cdot y + 7 \\) when divided by 3:\n\n\\[ (15 \\cdot y + 7) \\equiv 7 \\pmod{3} \\]\n\\[ \\Rightarrow 15 \\cdot y + 7 \\equiv 0 + 7 \\equiv 1 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\n\nThis means \\( 15 \\cdot y + 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\).\n\nNow let's find the remainder of \\( 15 \\cdot y + 7 \\) when divided by 5:\n\n\\[ (15 \\cdot y + 7) \\equiv 7 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\\[ \\Rightarrow 15 \\cdot y + 7 \\equiv 0 + 2 \\equiv 2 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nThis means \\( 15 \\cdot y + 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\).\n\nTo find the integers \\( y \\) that satisfy both equations, we can use the greatest common divisor :\n\n\\[ \\gcd(3,5) = 1 \\]\n\nIf \\( y \\equiv 0 \\pmod{1} \\), finding integers \\( y \\) that make both equations true, there are values \\( y = 5k + 2 \\) and \\( y = 3m + 1 \\) that satisfy Equation 2.\n\nLastly, we can calculate \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ x = 15y + r \\]\nwhere \\( 0 \\leq r < 15 \\) and \\( y \\) makes both conditions above true.\n\nSince we are given Equation 1, one of the remainders when dividing by 3 or 5 needs to be computed. We found Equation 1 has \\( r_1 \\) = 1 or 2 depending on \\( y \\) (the value of whatever satisfies the equation).\n\nFor Equation 3, the sum of the remainders is \\( 1 + 2 = 3 \\).\n\nFinal answer:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
