      "text": "Let's start by setting up the problem mathematically. We know that when an integer \\( n \\) is divided by 15, the remainder is 7. This can be written as:\n\n\\[ n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{15} \\]\n\nWe want to find the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5 and then sum them up. Let's express \\( n \\) in terms of its remainder when divided by 15:\n\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\n\nNow, let's find the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5.\n\n**Finding the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3:**\n\nSince \\( n = 15k + 7 \\), we can substitute \\( 15k + 7 \\) for \\( n \\):\n\n\\[ n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{3} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder of 7 when divided by 3:\n\n\\[ 7 \\div 3 = 2 \\text{ R } 1 \\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[ n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\n\n**Finding the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5:**\n\nSimilarly, since \\( n = 15k + 7 \\), we can substitute \\( 15k + 7 \\) for \\( n \\):\n\n\\[ n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder of 7 when divided by 5:\n\n\\[ 7 \\div 5 = 1 \\text{ R } 2 \\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[ n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\n**Summing the remainders:**\n\nNow, we need to sum the remainders of \\( n \\) when divided by 3 and by 5:\n\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the remainders when the same integer is divided by 3 and by 5 is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{3}\\]",
